From bf25b4c96af71bd48eef75720e685e5b906ad863 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emmanuel Kimathi <kimathi@example.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 05:41:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix missing migration and broken student-timetable feature

Your build failure:
  NodeNotFoundError: Migration notifications.0002_..._automated_sender
  dependencies reference nonexistent parent node
  ('timetable', '0002_timetableslot_lecturer_name_text')

Root cause: apps/schedule/services.py already references
slot.lecturer_name_text as a fallback display name, and the
notifications migration already depends on a timetable migration
named after that field - but the field itself was never actually
added to the TimetableSlot model. Almost certainly dropped during a
merge conflict with an earlier 'make lecturer nullable' migration
that touched the same model around the same time, on a different
branch - the code and the migration reference survived, the actual
field definition didn't.

Fixes:
- Added TimetableSlot.lecturer_name_text (CharField, blank) and
  generated its migration (0003_timetableslot_lecturer_name_text,
  since 0002 was already taken) - repointed the notifications
  migration's dependency at this real migration instead of the
  missing one.

While fixing this I found the entire student personal-timetable
feature (apps/schedule/services.py, powers 'my schedule' for
students) was separately, completely broken:
- Used slot.day everywhere (queryset ordering, grouping, filtering,
  and the API response) - TimetableSlot has no such field, only
  day_of_week. Every call to generate_for_user() would have thrown
  FieldError on the very first query.
- Even after fixing the field name: day_of_week is stored lowercase
  ('mon') but DAY_ORDER and the response dict use uppercase ('MON'),
  so real slot data was silently landing in stray lowercase dict
  keys instead of the intended MON-SAT buckets, which stayed empty.

Verified end-to-end against a fresh database: full migration graph
applies cleanly, and a real call to generate_for_user() with two
overlapping slots (one with a linked lecturer, one using the
lecturer_name_text fallback, one with neither) correctly groups both
into MON, correctly detects the time conflict, and correctly
resolves the lecturer display name for all three cases.
